            "finding_occurrence_id": "35fb72fff0de4b0e8df84971698a98a5",
            "title": "transform() breaking change: raises on non-numeric input and alters numeric string output",
            "category": "correctness",
            "path": "app/review_25.py",
            "start_line": 4,
            "end_line": 4,
            "trigger_condition": "Calling transform(None), transform(\"abc\"), or transform(1.1) under the new implementation.",
            "impact": "",
            "explanation": "The change replaces `str(value)` with `str(apply(value))`, where `apply(value)` is `Decimal(value).quantize(Decimal('0.01'))` (contracts/rule_25.py:2-3). The old implementation returned `str(value)` for any object, a total function. The new implementation calls `Decimal(value)` before stringification, so non-numeric inputs now raise instead of returning a string: e.g. `transform(None)` or `transform(\"abc\")` raises decimal.InvalidOperation. Numeric string output also changes: `transform(1.1)` previously returned \"1.1\" but now `Decimal(1.1)` exposes the binary float (1.1000000000000000888...) and quantize returns \"1.10\". Negative half values under default ROUND_HALF_EVEN produce \"-0.00\". Repo-wide reference search finds no callers and no validation/migration evidence that all users have adapted, so this is an unproven breaking change to the public `transform` contract, contradicting the PR claim of preserving externally observable behavior.",
            "suggestion": "Restore total-function behavior: only apply quantize for Decimal/numeric inputs and fall back to `str(value)` on invalid input, or wrap in try/except (InvalidOperation, TypeError, ValueError). Also decide and document rounding mode to avoid unexpected \"-0.00\" for negative half values.",
            "status": "suppressed",
            "needs_evidence": false
          }
