            "category": "correctness",
            "path": "app/review_30.py",
            "start_line": 4,
            "end_line": 4,
            "trigger_condition": "Calling `transform` with a 2-element sequence whose elements are not str/bytes-like (e.g. ints) or with a sequence whose length is not exactly 2.",
            "impact": "",
            "explanation": "The old implementation `value[0] == value[1]` returns a bool for any subscriptable value with at least 2 elements and for any comparable element types (e.g. `transform([1, 2])` -> False, `transform([1, 2, 3])` -> False). The new implementation delegates to `apply`, which (1) unpacks `value` with `supplied, expected = value`, requiring exactly 2 items, and (2) calls `hmac.compare_digest`, which raises TypeError for non-str/non-bytes-like types and returns False only for supported types. Thus `transform([1, 2])` now raises `TypeError: comparing unsupported types`, and `transform([1, 2, 3])` raises `ValueError: too many values to unpack`. This contradicts the stated goal of preserving externally observable behavior. No in-repo caller or validation was found to constrain inputs to str/bytes 2-element sequences.",
            "suggestion": "Either keep the original `==` for non-string/bytes inputs and only use `hmac.compare_digest` when both elements are str or bytes-like, or add explicit type/length validation and migrate all callers. If the public contract is intentionally narrowed, that is a breaking API change and requires caller updates/documentation.",
